---
import BlogArticle from '../../components/BlogArticle.astro';
import BlogCode from '../../components/BlogCode.astro';

const contents = [
  { id: 'taxes', label: 'What a cloud-backed PR check costs' },
  { id: 'step', label: 'One step in the job you already have' },
  { id: 'workflow', label: 'The complete workflow' },
  { id: 'split', label: 'What runs where' },
];
const stepOnly = `      - name: Start LocalCloud
        uses: agent-local-cloud/setup-localcloud@3f8bd6df58cfd3cf4cd29c46f3449c7e6465f64a
        with:
          image: agentcloud/localcloud:ci
          console: 'false'
          services: gcs,pubsub,bigquery
          project-id: localcloud-ci`;
const ghWorkflow = `name: pr-local-cloud-integration-tests
on:
  pull_request:
  push:
    branches: [main]
permissions:
  contents: read
jobs:
  integration:
    runs-on: ubuntu-24.04
    timeout-minutes: 15
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v7.0.1
      - uses: actions/setup-python@v7.0.0
        with:
          python-version: '3.12'
      - name: Install existing dependencies
        run: python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
      - name: Start LocalCloud
        uses: agent-local-cloud/setup-localcloud@3f8bd6df58cfd3cf4cd29c46f3449c7e6465f64a
        with:
          image: agentcloud/localcloud:ci
          console: 'false'
          services: gcs,pubsub,bigquery
          project-id: localcloud-ci
      - name: Run existing integration tests
        run: python -m pytest tests/integration/`;
---

<BlogArticle
  slug="zero-secret-gcp-ci-github-actions"
  kind="Walkthrough"
  deck="Integration tests on pull requests usually mean a service-account key in your CI secrets and a shared staging project that two builds can corrupt at once. Start the cloud inside the job and you need neither."
  primary={{ href: '/docs/github-actions/', label: 'GitHub Actions guide' }}
  secondary={{ href: '/gcp-integration-testing/', label: 'GCP integration testing in CI' }}
  {contents}
  outcome="A pull-request check that runs your existing integration tests against a fresh local Google Cloud, with no cloud credentials in the repository."
  needs="a repository on GitHub with integration tests that use the Google Cloud client libraries"
  next={{ posts: ['localcloud-for-open-source', 'tdd-google-cloud-watch-mode'], doc: { href: '/docs/github-actions/', label: 'GitHub Actions guide: fork policy, service containers and GitLab' } }}
>
  <section>
    <h2 id="taxes">What a cloud-backed PR check costs</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Pointing pull-request tests at a real project looks like the honest option. It comes with three bills.</p>
    <ul class="site-list mt-5">
      <li><strong>Credentials.</strong> A key or a workload identity binding has to live where CI can reach it, and you have to decide what pull requests from forks are allowed to do with it. Usually the answer is nothing, so outside contributors get no integration tests.</li>
      <li><strong>Collisions.</strong> Two builds writing to one staging bucket fail each other at random. The reruns teach the team to distrust red.</li>
      <li><strong>Charges.</strong> Every push pays for queries, messages and storage operations that exist only to be deleted.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="step">One step in the job you already have</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">The <code>setup-localcloud</code> Action starts LocalCloud on the runner, waits until the services you named are ready, and exports the SDK endpoints to the steps after it. Add it before your test step:</p>
    <BlogCode label="The step to add" code={stepOnly} copy="copy-blog-ci-step" />
    <p class="mt-4">Your tests, lockfile and client code stay as they are. The Google Cloud clients read the exported endpoints and talk to the runner instead of the internet. When the job ends, the Action removes the runtime.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="workflow">The complete workflow</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">If you are starting from nothing, this is a whole workflow for a Python project. Change the setup and test commands to match your repository, and list only the services your tests use.</p>
    <BlogCode label=".github/workflows/localcloud.yml" code={ghWorkflow} copy="copy-blog-ci-workflow" />
    <p class="mt-4">There is no <code>secrets.</code> reference anywhere in that file. It runs the same way for a maintainer's branch and a first-time contributor's fork, and every run gets a container and a project nobody else has touched.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="split">What runs where</h2>
    <table class="blog-compare mt-5">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">When</th><th scope="col">Against</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Integration tests</th><td>Every pull request and push</td><td>LocalCloud on the runner</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Deploy verification</th><td>Main branch or release tags</td><td>Your staging project</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">IAM and quota behaviour</th><td>Before a release</td><td>Your staging project</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="mt-4">The split moves the frequent, noisy work off the shared project and keeps the cloud for the checks only the cloud can answer. Staging gets quieter, and the credentials that reach it are used by one trusted workflow instead of every pull request.</p>
  </section>
</BlogArticle>
